core","color":"ffee88","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":27,"created_at":"2025-07-30T19:44:58Z","updated_at":"2025-07-31T20:20:55Z","closed_at":"2025-07-31T19:16:22Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nReproducible in 4.4.1.stable, 4.5.beta3\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.beta3 - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1660 SUPER (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.7652) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-10700F CPU @ 2.90GHz (16 threads) - 15.92 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nIf you use `Node.duplicate` on a node that is an instantiated scene, the resulting copy is always an instantiated scene, even if you leave `DUPLICATE_USE_INSTANTIATION` out of the flags passed into the function. This is unexpected, and makes it impossible to achieve the programmatic equivalent of the \"Make Local\" action on a node. i.e. severing the connection with the source scene.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nOpen `test.tscn` in the attached project, and select the root node. Click on the `Copy` button in the inspector. Notice that the resulting object is an instantiated scene, despite the code passing `0` as the duplicate flags.\n\n<img width=\"378\" height=\"156\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/75b7d8dd-850d-4f1d-8c50-b9ad2bb6b438\" />\n<img width=\"263\" height=\"128\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8066fa53-beda-4752-bbf5-5562fc96b320\" />\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[bug-report-2.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/21516925/bug-report-2.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"